How to retain information using a stack in Java


In the second piece, I need to implement a stack to keep track of the information. The stack should be able to hold multiple items and allow for easy addition and removal of items.

Here is a simple implementation of a stack in Java:

```java
class Stack<T> {
    private final int capacity;
    private final int size;

    public Stack(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.size = 0;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }

    public T top() {
        return elements[--size];
    }

    public void push(T item) {
        elements.add(item);
        size++;
    }

    public T pop() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return elements[--size];
    }

    public T peek() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return elements[0];
    }
}
```

This implementation works fine for small stacks, but it has a limitation: it uses a fixed-size array for the elements, which means it can only hold a limited number of elements. If you need to handle larger data sets

