The explanation is simple: you're modifying the same array in two different places. When you change an element in one array, the change is reflected in the other array because they are the same underlying data structure. This is a common issue when working with arrays in Java. 

In Java, arrays are mutable, meaning they can be changed after they are created. When you assign the same array to both `original` and `changed`, any changes you make to one will be reflected in the other. This is because both `original` and `changed` point to the same underlying array in memory.

Here's a quick example to illustrate this:

```java
private String original[] = new String[100];
private String changed[] = new String[100];

// Assigning values to both arrays
original[0] = "Hello";
changed[0] = "World";

// Changing a value in one array
changed[0] = "New value";

// Accessing the values
System.out.println(original[0]); // Outputs: New value
System.out.println(changed[0]); // Outputs: New value
```

As you can see, changing a value in `changed` also changes the corresponding value in `original`, because they are the same array.

