Based on the first piece, how can I ensure that the first element of std::vector v always gets the same address?

The solution to this problem is to ensure that the vector is resized with the same size as the number of elements you intend to store. Here is the corrected code:

#include <memory>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main()
{
    std::vector<int> v;

    for (int i=0; i<10; ++i)
    {
        int b = i;

        v.push_back(b);

        std::cout << std::addressof(v[0]) << std::endl;
    }

    // Resizing the vector to match the number of elements
    v.resize(10);

    return 0;
}

