samples.guided_completion:
Dispose is a method that is part of the IDisposable interface. It is used to ensure that all resources are properly released when an object is no longer needed. The primary purpose of Dispose is to allow an object to clean up any unmanaged resources it holds, such as database connections, file handles, network connections, and other resources that are not automatically released when the object is garbage collected.

When a class implements IDisposable, it should call Dispose on all resources it owns. However, if a class does not own any resources, calling Dispose is not strictly necessary. The harm in not calling Dispose is that it can lead to resource leaks, which can cause performance issues and potentially lead to crashes or other problems.

In the case of ASP.NET web forms controls, the .NET runtime automatically manages the lifecycle of these controls. You do not need to call Dispose on these controls directly. However, if you want to ensure that Dispose is called, you can use the using statement, which ensures that Dispose is called automatically when the using block is exited.

