There is no advantage to "declaring the variable in the global scope" and it may not even be possible in R depending on what you mean by that. You certainly could use the second form. The action that causes duplicate or even triplicate copies of an object is assignment.

samples.guided_completion:
The function g() would then look like this:

g <- function (a) { a+y }

f <- function(x) {
    y <- 3 # but in my program y is very large
    g(x)
}

Now, when you call g(), it will pass both a and y to the function g(). This is memory efficient because R will only store the values once, regardless of how many times they are used.

However, if you want to avoid the assignment altogether, you can use the apply function:

g <- function (a) { a + global$y }

f <- function(x) {
    global$y <- 3 # but in my program y is very large
    g(x)
}

In this case, the global$y is a named vector that holds the value of y, and the function g() simply adds it to a. This is also memory efficient because R will only store the value once, regardless of how many times it is used.

So, the standard way to pass large objects in R without copying them is to use apply or similar functions, or to use the second form with named vectors.

